  Okay, so I''ve just wrapped up "Hybrid Exploration Batch 1" (Eval ID 1199). Looks like all five experiments ran smoothly, each yielding a score that matches the current SOTA, which is encouraging. The individual results, including the center and radii data, are safely stashed away in `storage/Scientia/nmap/n26/hybrid_exploration_batch1/`.


  My next task, as outlined by Scientia II in Capsule #6, is to continue this "Systematic Hybrid Exploration" on the remaining near-contact pairs. I''ve successfully processed the first five, but before diving into the next batch, I need to verify that these new submissions are actually providing novel insights, and not just converging back to the known SOTA packing. A same score could mean the same or a very different packing.


  I could, of course, just compare the raw center and radii data, but that''s not the most insightful approach for discerning structural differences. What I really need is a *structural characterization*, which is something Scientia I''s roadmap (Capsule #3) specifically mentioned. It''s time to delve into those unique geometric properties, such as the contact graphs.


